    <item>
      <title>A Brief Visual Guide to Functions in Assembly (draft)</title>
      <link>https://vachanvy.github.io/posts/asmfunccalls/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 14 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://vachanvy.github.io/posts/asmfunccalls/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;getting-started&#34;&gt;Getting Started&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;!-- take a simple simple program --&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-C&#34; data-lang=&#34;C&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;() {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;~&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;70&lt;/span&gt;);&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-asm&#34; data-lang=&#34;asm&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;.globl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;main:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;pushq&lt;/span&gt; %rbp&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;movq&lt;/span&gt; %rsp, %rbp&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# needs to be double word aligned, so 16 bytes&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;subq&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;$16&lt;/span&gt;, %rsp&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# highly inefficient/unoptimized, but just used to illustrate an example&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# modern compilers would optimize this away...&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# this part is obvious... doing ~(-70)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;movl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;$70&lt;/span&gt;, -&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;(%rbp)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;negl&lt;/span&gt; -&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;(%rbp)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;movl&lt;/span&gt; -&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;(%rbp), %r10d&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;movl&lt;/span&gt; %r10d, -&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;(%rbp)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;notl&lt;/span&gt; -&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;(%rbp)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;movl&lt;/span&gt; -&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;(%rbp), %eax&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;movq&lt;/span&gt; %rbp, %rsp&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;popq&lt;/span&gt; %rbp&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;ret&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;.section&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;.note.GNU-stack&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;,&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;@progbits&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;!-- explain rsp and rbp --&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;RSP register (64-bit Stack Pointer) points to the top of the stack. It grows to lower memory addresses as data is pushed onto the stack. In many books, it shown growing upwards, but in reality, it grows downwards.&#xA;RBP register (64-bit Base Pointer) points to the base of the current caller&amp;rsquo;s stack frame.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Writing a Lexer in Pure C (draft)</title>
      <link>https://vachanvy.github.io/posts/clexer/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 11 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://vachanvy.github.io/posts/clexer/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;So I wrote a lexer in pure C for my &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/VachanVY/nanocc&#34;&gt;Nano C Compiler (nanocc)&lt;/a&gt;. In this post, I&amp;rsquo;ll walk you through the design and implementation of the lexer, explaining the key components and decisions made along the way. I&amp;rsquo;m assuming you have basic knowledge of regular expressions and finite automata.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;ve only implemented a subset of C (no structs, pointers, arrays, etc.) for now, but the lexer can be easily extended to support more features in the future.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
